WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.609
You may have heard a lot about DNA.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:12.330
DNA stand for Deoxyribonucleic Acid.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:17.020
Individual pieces of DNA formed the gene,
and then collectively they comprise the entire

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.020
genome.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.750
DNA was identified about 100 years ago by
Friedrich Miescher, who was a chemist in Germany

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:24.750
at the time.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:30.199
He was trying to get cell nuclei out of bandages
used in hospitals, which are full of white

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:32.439
blood cells that contain a lot of DNA.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:37.040
And he wanted to know what a chemical composition
was.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.199
So he found that there was a lot of this stuff
that he called, at the time, nuclein and it

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:46.690
took a very long time to realize that this
nuclein actually was important for life.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.800
People thought it was just some kind of structural
molecule that held the nuclei together.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.340
It is not until Watson and Crick figured out
what the structure of DNA was, that people

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.549
realized that it was a molecule that could
carry information.

00:01:00.549 --> 00:01:05.510
Inside the cell, you will most commonly find
double-stranded DNA, in which two strands

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.210
intertwine to form a double helix.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:13.770
The most common form of the DNA double helix
is also called B-form DNA.

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:18.280
We can unwind the double helix so that we
can see the chemical structure inside.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.560
Each strand is a polynucleotide, meaning the
strand is made up of many individual units

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.590
called nucleotides.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.899
A nucleotide has three components: the five-carbon
sugar, a phosphate group, and one of four

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:36.079
possible nitrogenous bases - adenine, guanine,
thymine, and cytosine.

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:41.149
The nitrogenous base is always attached at
the 1’ carbon of the sugar.

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:45.590
If we count from there, we can see that there
is a phosphate between the 5’ carbon of

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.610
one sugar and the 3' carbon of the neighboring
sugar.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:54.810
The sugar is called deoxyribose because it
is missing a hydroxyl group at the 2' carbon

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.569
which is present in ribose.

00:01:57.569 --> 00:02:03.210
Because of this, nucleotides in DNA are called
deoxynucleotides.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:07.869
Nucleotides attach to each other in the DNA
strand by phosphodiester bonds.

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:13.760
The phosphate group of one nucleotide binds
to the 3' oxygen of the neighboring nucleotide.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.200
Thus, we can see that the sugars and phosphate
groups make up the DNA backbone.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.360
The carbon numbering is key to describing
the directionality of the DNA strand, 5' to

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.940
3'.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:31.610
Looking within the sugars, there is an intrinsic
orientation difference between the two strands.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:36.310
On the top strand, you can see that the 5'
carbon of each sugar is on the left and the

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.920
3' carbon is on the right.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.030
The opposite is true for the bottom strand.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:47.200
Reading left to right, that makes the top
strand orientation 5' to 3' and the bottom

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.810
strand orientation 3' to 5'.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.710
Keep in mind that this double-stranded DNA
is still a double helix and we have simplified

00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:01.580
the representation by flattening and unwinding
the helix here to better see the atomic structure.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:06.730
Although the nucleotides come together through
covalent bonds in the backbone, the two DNA

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:11.270
strands interact through non-covalent hydrogen
bonds between the bases.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:17.080
Each base forms multiple hydrogen bonds with
its complementary base on the opposite strand.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:22.000
Bound together by hydrogen bonds, each unit
is called a base pair.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.170
The hydrogen bonding contributes to the specificity
of base pairing.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:31.850
Thymine preferentially pairs with adenine
through two hydrogen bonds and cytosine preferentially

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.210
pairs with guanine through three hydrogen
bonds.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:40.820
Thymine and cytosine are called pyrimidines,
characterized by their single ring structure,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:45.010
and adenine and guanine are called purines,
which have double rings.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:50.980
The geometry of the AT or TA and GC or CG
base pairs is the same, allowing for symmetry

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.560
and base stacking in the helix.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.730
This mostly has to do with the distance between
the backbones and the angles to which the

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.600
bases attach to the backbone.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.030
Other base pairs, like GT, for example, do
not have the same geometry, cannot form strong

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.450
hydrogen bonds, and disturb the helix.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.700
The double helix structure of DNA is highly
regular.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.970
Each turn of the helix measures approximately
ten base pairs.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:22.400
In addition to the hydrogen bonding between
the bases, the stacking of the bases also

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.620
stabilizes the double helix structure.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.419
These - interactions form when the aromatic
rings of the bases stack next to each other

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:33.210
and share electron probabilities.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.909
The regularity of the helical structure forms
two repeating and alternating spaces, called

00:04:37.909 --> 00:04:40.490
the major and minor grooves.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:45.669
These grooves act as base pair recognition
and binding sites for proteins.

00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:50.180
The major groove contains base pair specific
information while the minor groove is largely

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.720
base pair nonspecific.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.900
This is because of the patterns of hydrogen
bond acceptors and donors that proteins can

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.370
interact with in the grooves.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:04.020
In this way, the DNA can be acted upon in
either a sequence specific or non-sequence

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.870
specific manner, allowing proteins to position
themselves correctly in the genome to carry

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.990
out their designated tasks.

